'''''Pittosporum ferrugineum''''', commonly known as the '''rusty pittosporum''' or '''rusty-leaved pittosporum''', is an evergreen plant in the family [[Pittosporaceae]] native to [[Malesia]], [[Papuasia]], the [[Northern Territory]] and [[Queensland]].

==Description==
''Pittosporum ferrugineum'' is a shrub or small tree growing to around 10&nbsp;m high. The new growth (twigs, leaves and flowers) is densely covered in fine rusty-brown hairs − giving rise to the common name − but becoming less hairy as it matures. Leaves are dull green, [[Glossary of leaf morphology#elliptic|elliptic]] to narrow-elliptic, 6–13&nbsp;cm long by 2–5&nbsp;cm wide, on a [[Petiole (botany)|petiole]] 1.5–2.5&nbsp;cm long.{{R|RFK|FOA}}

The [[inflorescence]]s are clusters of flowers about 4&nbsp;cm wide,{{R|NPSINGAPORE}} produced in the [[Glossary of botanical terms#axil|leaf axils]] at any time of the year. The fragrant flowers have five petals, measure about 10&nbsp;mm long and wide, and are white, cream or yellow in colour.{{R|COOPER}}

The dull yellow or orange fruit is a [[Dehiscence (botany)|dehiscent]] 2-valved [[capsule (fruit)|capsule]] containing up to 16 small red seeds aggregated into a sticky ball.{{R|RFK|FOA|COOPER}}

==Taxonomy==
This species was first described − albeit very briefly − and named in 1811 by the English botanist [[William Townsend Aiton]] in the book [[Hortus Kewensis]].{{R|BHL}}

===Subspecies===
There are two [[subspecies]] of this taxon recognised by [[Plants of the World Online]] (POWO) − [[Pittosporum ferrugineum ssp. laxiflorum|''P.f. laxiflorum'']] [[Richard Schodde|{{small|Schodde}}]], and the [[Autonym (botany)|autonym]] [[Pittosporum ferrugineum ssp. ferrugineum|''P.f. ferrugineum'']].{{R|POWO}} A former subspecies − ''P.f. linifolium'' was elevated to species status in 2017 under the combination ''[[Pittosporum tinifolium]]''.{{R|CAYZER}}

===Etymology===
The [[Botanical name#Binary name|species epithet]] ''ferrugineum'' is from the Latin [[wikt:ferrugineus|''ferrugineus'']], meaning "of the colour of rust", and was given to this species by Aiton in reference to the dense red-brown hairs on the plant.{{R|COOPER}}

==Distribution and habitat==
The rusty-leaved pittosporum is found in [[Malesia]], [[Papuasia]] and northern Australia, with records from Malaysia, Borneo, Sulawesi, the Philippines, the Caroline Islands, Java, Sumatra, [[Lesser Sunda Islands]], the [[Maluku Islands]], New Guinea, the Bismark Archipelago, the Solomon Islands, [[Christmas Island]], the Northern Territory and Queensland (north of Cooktown). It has been introduced to Mauritius, Sri Lanka, Cambodia and Vietnam.{{R|POWO|GBIF}}

This species is known to inhabit drier rainforest types such as [[Seasonal tropical forest|monsoon forest]], [[Littoral zone|beach forest]] and the margins of [[mangrove forest]].{{R|RFK|NPSINGAPORE}}

==Ecology==
The fruit of this species is eaten by [[metallic starling]]s (''Aplonis metallica'').{{R|COOPER}} Flowers attract a variety of birds and butterflies.{{R|NPSINGAPORE}}

==Conservation==
This species is listed by the [[Department of Environment and Science (Queensland)|Queensland Department of Environment and Science]] as [[Least-concern species|least concern]].{{R|DESQLD}} {{As of|2023|02|21}}, it has not been assessed by the [[IUCN]].

